lesson chemistry-the-rock-cycle-and-the-gorge v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=4 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The rock cycle and the gorge's layers"
  goal "Follow a rock round the rock cycle, and read the order its layers were laid down in from a gorge's walls."

  look {
    scene 29x14 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=2 align=end {
        waterfall fall drops=2 rainbow=1
        strata s layers=5 names=1
      }
    }
    say """
      The river at the waterfall gorge has cut down through five layers of rock. Each layer was
      laid down as mud or sand on top of the one before, so the bottom layer is the oldest. Rock
      does not stay the same for ever. Rain and rivers wear it into sediment, sediment is pressed
      and cemented into sedimentary rock, heat and pressure change it into metamorphic rock, and
      rock that melts into magma cools into igneous rock. This is the rock cycle.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice chem.rock-kind count=1 seed=1
    level easy {
      worked chem.cycle-process b=2 r=0 level=medium
    }
    practice chem.cycle-process count=2 seed=1
    practice chem.cycle-hidden count=1 seed=1
    practice chem.gorge-layers count=2 seed=1 hard-seed=5
    level hard {
      show chem.cycle-hidden h=0
    }
  }
  story {
    show chem.rock-density-id k=1 v=10 b=50 easy-k=0 hard-v=20 hard-b=30
    show chem.rock-density-id k=0 v=20 b=30 easy-v=10 hard-v=30 hard-b=50
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chem.why-crystals k=0 r=1 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chem.cycle-steps k=1 all=0 easy-k=0 easy-all=1 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Rock goes round a cycle: worn to sediment, pressed into sedimentary rock, changed by heat and pressure, melted to magma and cooled to igneous rock. Lower layers are older."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is the rock cycle: the three kinds of rock and what turns one into another, and that a
    layer laid down on another is younger than it, so a gorge's walls are read from the bottom up.
    Granite and basalt are igneous, cooled from melted rock; sandstone, limestone, shale and chalk
    are sedimentary, laid down in layers; marble and slate are metamorphic, changed by heat and
    pressure without melting. The wrong ideas to watch for are that rock never changes, and that
    metamorphic rock was melted (then it would be igneous). The story finds a stone's rock by its
    density, as in the density lesson. The two-star question asks why granite has big crystals and
    basalt tiny ones, or how limestone became marble. The three-star question counts the fewest
    arrows from one box of the cycle to another, trying every route. If the child reads the layers
    top down as oldest first, stay here another day with a pile of books laid one on another. Off
    the page: look at a wall or a cliff and find layers; press damp sand in a cup and see it hold.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level one missing arrow is worked in the teacher's pen, the layers ask which came first, the stones are 10 or 20 cm³, the two-star asks about granite's crystals, and the three-star has the shortcuts drawn."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the layers ask which of two is older and which came just before another, the stones are 20 or 30 cm³, the two-star asks how limestone became marble, and the three-star has only the loop, so the way round is longer."
  }
}
